I think that it's easier sort of  narrative and it's a more comfortable  narrative to push that there was this  that southerners or southern states were  just pushing for for more rights as  states and and it's a deeply American  narrative because obviously the United  States is a federation of states and  each states has its own set of policies  and then you have a national government  with its own set of policy so it seems  sort of inherent in the American story  but northern states historically have  advocated policies that have been  challenged by federal power and so this  is this is a national issue in terms of  the relationship between states and the  federal government but what wasn't a  national issue were the rights of  slaveholders right were the rights of  segregationist and the ways in which  they felt at times that the federal  government was not supporting them by  allowing them to continue to spread  throughout the United States in terms of  slaveholders and and and and obviously  enslaved people each of these books did  not talk about the role that white  supremacy played in the ideology of the  Confederate States of America and so  that's how I think people today could be  like well what's wrong with the  Confederacy they were just advocating  for states rights they were just  advocating for freedom against Northern  Aggression those who were enslaved I  think so and I think so to give an  example the vice president of the  Confederacy Alexander Stephens in 1862  gave what became known as the  cornerstone speech which to me if  there's a speech that really speaks to  the principles and the ideology of the  Confederate States of America it's this  speech this speech wasn't quoted in any  of these books in that speech he stated  that the Confederacy is founded on the  great truth that the Negro is not equal  to the white man and slavery  subordination to the superior race is  is natural and normal condition so again  I think what if we use the term slavery  and slave holders rights as opposed to  states rights part of the problem with  the term states rights is that this was  the term that the Confederate states  that later segregationist and even some  slaveholders utilized to hide that they  were really fighting for the rights of  slaveholders
 

